<BaseLayout title="Build and test" description="Prototype requirements, validation measurements and current implementation status for the O2AT v0.2 engineering proposal.">
  <div class="wrap page-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Engineering programme</p><h1>What a credible prototype must show.</h1><p class="lead">The v0.2 specification is a proposal. This page separates its present analytical evidence from the hardware and interoperability work still required.</p></div>
  <section class="section"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Implementation status</p><h2>No public O2AT prototype is claimed.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The O2AT v0.2 document defines proposed framing, packetisation, capability negotiation, receiver credits and clock states. This site publishes its calculations and a labelled illustrative model. No public reference implementation, bitstream or hardware bridge has been identified for this release.</p><p>Existing playback reports, including the author’s reported DSD512 playback using LAiV equipment, concern that equipment and do not demonstrate an O2AT transport implementation.</p><div class="status-note"><strong>Status:</strong> specification draft published; hardware implementation and physical validation not demonstrated.</div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Prototype requirements</p><h2>Evidence to collect, not results to assume.</h2></div><div class="prose"><ul><li>Capture and decode both Ethernet directions. Verify the 12-byte header, sequence handling, FCS rejection and exact capability descriptor values.</li><li>Measure actual 1G-SX module interoperability, host SerDes/MAC behaviour, optical power margin, module power and loss/reacquisition.</li><li>Exercise PCM and native DSD packing with bit-exact vectors, channel order, group counters, malformed payloads and endpoint rejection.</li><li>Measure FIFO occupancy against packet delay, bursts, dropped frames, stalls and source rate error. Include data in flight in the credit bound.</li><li>Measure conversion-clock frequency, uncertainty, relative drift, phase noise, spurs and time error in both local and external-reference modes.</li><li>Interrupt and restore 10 MHz reference; verify declared holdover bounds, local mute/abort and fresh-session recovery.</li><li>Use a physical reference plane and instruments to document uncertainty, temperature and reproducible test configuration.</li><li>Publish independent encoder/decoder test vectors and confirm interoperability with at least two independently developed implementations.</li></ul></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">System boundaries</p><h2>Transport does not remove endpoint ceilings.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>A 1G transport calculation does not prove that a host USB controller, bridge, FPGA, DAC, I²S path or analogue output supports the same rate. The source paper explicitly separates the transport envelope from installed output capability.</p><p>For fixed-rate live legacy inputs, a finite FIFO and receiver credits cannot force an unpausable producer to run at the receiver’s rate indefinitely. A bridge requires a genuine clock-control mechanism, an explicit conversion strategy or a defined safe rejection condition.</p><p>ADAT and S/MUX bridging, preservation of legacy encoded formats and full metadata mapping are separate engineering scope. The paper treats them as future work.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Conformance evidence</p><h2>Release only against a named draft.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Any test result should identify the ODT hash, wire-version nibble, encoder/decoder revision, endpoint profile, instrument model and configuration, traffic conditions, environmental conditions, and raw output files needed to reproduce the claim.</p><p>Controlled testing of EtherType <code>0x88B5</code> is suitable only for an evaluation network. A commercial deployment requires the identifier and interoperability programme to be resolved.</p><p><a href="/contribute/">Review the open test questions →</a></p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section section-top"><div class="wrap split"><div><p class="eyebrow">Hardware identification</p><h2>Use the compact mark at device scale.</h2></div><div class="prose hardware-mark"><figure><img src="/brand/o2at_baseline_black.svg" width="280" height="79" alt="Approved O2AT baseline wordmark for compact hardware identification"><figcaption>Approved baseline O2AT wordmark. Supplied geometry and spacing are retained. A fabricator must qualify its finished size and process on the intended material.</figcaption></figure><p>The outlined superscript O²AT mark is used on the website; this baseline version is reserved for compact device identification. Neither file specifies minimum manufacturing dimensions.</p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
